{
  "abstract": "We prove a restricted nine-color theorem for biplanar graphs whose vertices are partitioned into fifteen independent pairs. Each interface is empty, a perfect matching, or full, and full interfaces have disjoint pair labels. A ten-chromatic graph in this class would therefore need at least sixteen pairs. The proof separates geometry from a finite signed-cover problem. A sheet-coloring lemma colors the original graph whenever fewer than nine interfaces cross between two isomorphic sheets. Padding a hypothetical counterexample to the biplanar edge bound reduces it to supports with minimum weighted degree nine and a ten-critical subgraph of order 10, 14, or 15. Critical-support decompositions, rank-five matching arguments, degree-list coloring and signed-cycle equations leave 101,602 and 3,327,419 placements for zero and one full interface. Independent support generators reduce these to 67 and 152 marked orbits. We reconstruct 219 Boolean formulas and verify all refutations with binary DRAT proofs. Ten exceptional formulas are checked again after removing their sheet clause. Literal signed graphs provide ten-color lower-bound controls and expose why the sheet hypothesis is needed. The archive contains the generators, inventories, formula manifests, proofs, hashes and replay script. The theorem covers the zero- through seven-full-interface cases at fifteen pairs when the full interfaces are disjoint, while the unrestricted Earth–Moon problem remains open.",
